WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.025
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.325 --> 00:00:08.096
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.396 --> 00:00:15.762
Unit forty: Human Rights. In this unit, you will discuss the principles and protection of human rights.

4
00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.552
universal declaration.

5
00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:27.138
universal declaration means the 1948 UN document listing basic human rights.

6
00:00:27.438 --> 00:00:33.176
The Universal Declaration of Human Rights was adopted in 1948.

7
00:00:33.876 --> 00:00:37.457
Your turn. Say: universal declaration.

8
00:00:40.157 --> 00:00:41.901
universal declaration.

9
00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:44.301
freedom of expression.

10
00:00:45.001 --> 00:00:49.497
freedom of expression means the right to share opinions and information.

11
00:00:49.797 --> 00:00:53.572
Freedom of expression includes the right to criticise.

12
00:00:54.272 --> 00:00:57.412
Your turn. Say: freedom of expression.

13
00:01:00.112 --> 00:01:01.392
freedom of expression.

14
00:01:02.383 --> 00:01:03.744
right to education.

15
00:01:04.444 --> 00:01:09.021
right to education means every person's right to learn and go to school.

16
00:01:09.321 --> 00:01:12.226
The right to education applies to every child.

17
00:01:12.926 --> 00:01:16.554
Your turn. Say: right to education.

18
00:01:19.254 --> 00:01:20.801
right to education.

19
00:01:21.817 --> 00:01:23.236
due process rights.

20
00:01:23.936 --> 00:01:28.780
due process rights means rights to fair legal treatment, such as a fair trial.

21
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.519
Due process rights protect people from unfair punishment.

22
00:01:33.219 --> 00:01:36.533
Your turn. Say: due process rights.

23
00:01:39.233 --> 00:01:40.548
due process rights.

24
00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:43.155
human rights defender.

25
00:01:43.855 --> 00:01:47.909
human rights defender means a person who works to protect others' rights.

26
00:01:48.209 --> 00:01:51.172
Human rights defenders often face risks.

27
00:01:51.872 --> 00:01:55.268
Your turn. Say: human rights defender.

28
00:01:57.968 --> 00:01:59.503
human rights defender.

29
00:02:00.517 --> 00:02:01.808
civil liberties.

30
00:02:02.508 --> 00:02:05.994
civil liberties means basic freedoms protected by law.

31
00:02:06.294 --> 00:02:09.117
Civil liberties include freedom of movement.

32
00:02:09.817 --> 00:02:13.364
Your turn. Say: civil liberties.

33
00:02:16.064 --> 00:02:17.378
civil liberties.

34
00:02:18.383 --> 00:02:19.837
freedom of assembly.

35
00:02:20.537 --> 00:02:23.895
freedom of assembly means the right to gather peacefully.

36
00:02:24.195 --> 00:02:27.031
Freedom of assembly allows peaceful protest.

37
00:02:27.731 --> 00:02:31.277
Your turn. Say: freedom of assembly.

38
00:02:33.977 --> 00:02:35.431
freedom of assembly.

39
00:02:36.417 --> 00:02:38.068
non-discrimination.

40
00:02:38.768 --> 00:02:43.426
non-discrimination means treating everyone equally regardless of identity.

41
00:02:43.726 --> 00:02:48.048
Non-discrimination is a core human rights principle.

42
00:02:48.748 --> 00:02:52.828
Your turn. Say: non-discrimination.

43
00:02:55.528 --> 00:02:56.994
non-discrimination.

44
00:02:57.983 --> 00:03:00.215
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:00.515 --> 00:03:04.500
Ms. Sarah, where does the idea of universal human rights come from?

46
00:03:05.783 --> 00:03:13.193
Formally, from the Universal Declaration of 1948, adopted after the Second World War.

47
00:03:14.483 --> 00:03:17.783
My friends sometimes say rights are a Western idea.

48
00:03:19.083 --> 00:03:24.310
That's a common view, but the drafting committee included members from several continents.

49
00:03:25.617 --> 00:03:27.802
Which rights matter most, in your opinion?

50
00:03:29.117 --> 00:03:34.312
They're indivisible. However, the right to education enables all the others.

51
00:03:35.617 --> 00:03:38.406
I'd say freedom of expression is pretty key too.

52
00:03:39.717 --> 00:03:46.258
Indeed. In formal terms, it is considered essential to accountability and participation.

53
00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:51.163
Grammar focus: Register in formal discussion.

54
00:03:51.463 --> 00:03:55.738
Serious topics usually require a formal register.

55
00:03:56.038 --> 00:04:02.775
Informally, freedom of expression is pretty key; formally, it is considered essential.

56
00:04:03.075 --> 00:04:05.957
It is widely held that rights are universal.

57
00:04:06.257 --> 00:04:12.935
In essays and presentations on serious topics, choose formal, precise vocabulary.

58
00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:16.939
Now read and listen. Rights for Everyone.

59
00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:23.790
In 1948, the United Nations adopted the Universal Declaration of Human Rights.

60
00:04:25.083 --> 00:04:30.879
It was drafted by representatives from many regions, cultures and religious traditions.

61
00:04:32.183 --> 00:04:37.910
The declaration states that all human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights.

62
00:04:39.217 --> 00:04:45.059
It includes civil liberties, such as freedom of expression and freedom of assembly.

63
00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:51.740
It also recognises social rights, including the right to education and health.

64
00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:57.511
Non-discrimination is a principle that runs through the entire document.

65
00:04:58.817 --> 00:05:03.243
Human rights defenders around the world work to ensure these principles are respected.

66
00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:10.927
It is widely held that rights are indivisible: progress in one area supports progress in others.

67
00:05:12.217 --> 00:05:15.032
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:15.332 --> 00:05:17.563
The right to gather peacefully is blank.

69
00:05:17.863 --> 00:05:24.640
A, freedom of assembly. B, due process rights. C, non-discrimination.

70
00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:32.054
The answer is A: freedom of assembly.

71
00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:35.224
Which phrase is most formal?

72
00:05:35.524 --> 00:05:40.803
A, pretty key. B, a big deal. C, considered essential.

73
00:05:44.817 --> 00:05:47.327
The answer is C: considered essential.

74
00:05:48.817 --> 00:05:51.922
'It is widely blank that education is a right.'

75
00:05:52.222 --> 00:05:56.212
A, held. B, told. C, said us.

76
00:06:00.217 --> 00:06:02.356
The answer is A: held.

77
00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:07.301
Treating everyone equally regardless of identity is blank.

78
00:06:07.601 --> 00:06:13.286
A, civil liberties. B, non-discrimination. C, universal declaration.

79
00:06:17.283 --> 00:06:20.792
The answer is B: non-discrimination.

80
00:06:22.283 --> 00:06:26.361
According to the text, when was the Universal Declaration adopted?

81
00:06:26.661 --> 00:06:34.819
A, 1918. B, 1948. C, 1990.

82
00:06:38.817 --> 00:06:42.616
The answer is B: 1948.

83
00:06:44.117 --> 00:06:50.182
Now it's your turn. Part one: Which human right do you consider most important, and why?

84
00:06:55.482 --> 00:07:02.172
Part two: Answer in a formal register: give your choice, two reasons and an example.

85
00:07:08.472 --> 00:07:23.190
I consider the right to education fundamental. Firstly, it enables people to understand and claim their other rights; secondly, it reduces poverty. Free primary education, for example, has transformed opportunities for millions of children.

86
00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:26.047
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:31.583
Great work today! You learned eight new words and register in formal discussion.

88
00:07:31.883 --> 00:07:35.220
Today's value is respect. Thank the people who help us.

89
00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.301
See you in Unit forty-one. Goodbye!

90
00:07:38.601 --> 00:07:41.137
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

